NAME
    state-path - resolve a managed m state pathname

SYNOPSIS
    state-path <scope> <owner> <identity> <area> [<state-instance>]

DESCRIPTION
    state-path is the public resolver for managed mutable state. It prints the
    semantic pathname for the requested state location and does not create the
    directory, mutate selectors, select package versions, or canonicalize a
    selected binding target.

    On success exactly one absolute pathname followed by a newline is written to
    standard output.

OPERANDS
    <scope>
        system or user.

    <owner>
        sys, ai, or pkg.

    <identity>
        Owner-local state identity using a controlled owner-local name.

    <area>
        One of: conf, data, home, cache, log, run, tmp.

    <state-instance>
        Optional package State Instance. It is accepted only when owner is pkg.
        The resolved state identity becomes <identity>@!<state-instance>.

USER SCOPE
    If state/user/current is a symbolic link, user state is resolved through that
    semantic current pathname. Otherwise user state resolves under
    state/user/default.

    The user scope is an m namespace. It is not derived from the POSIX UID,
    username, HOME, hostname, or another host-account identifier.

PACKAGE STATE
    Package consumers use state-path rather than reconstructing the state tree.
    For example, the normal user-scoped package HOME is resolved with:

        state-path user pkg <package> home

ENVIRONMENT
    Requires a nonempty m_ROOT inherited from an initialized m environment.
    From an uninitialized host shell, invoke m state-path [arguments].


EXIT STATUS
    0   The pathname was resolved and written successfully.
    2   The invocation, scope, owner, identity, area, or State Instance is invalid.

    A lower-level output failure is returned as a non-zero status from the command.

EXAMPLES
    Resolve technical system configuration state:

        state-path system sys shell conf

    Resolve package user HOME:

        state-path user pkg nodejs home

    Resolve a package State Instance configuration area:

        state-path user pkg foo conf test

